      "text": "99,408.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Let me check my calculations again.\n\n114 multiplied by 2 is indeed 228.\n\n114 multiplied by 70 is 7,980.\n\n114 multiplied by 800 is 91,200.\n\nAdding these together: 228 plus 7,980 is 8,208. Then, 8,208 plus 91,200 equals 99,408.\n\nHmm, that still gives me 99,408. But I think I might have made a mistake in the multiplication steps. Let me try a different approach.\n\nAlternatively, I can break down 872 into 800, 70, and 2.\n\n114 multiplied by 800 is 91,200.\n\n114 multiplied by 70 is 7,980.\n\n114 multiplied by 2 is 228.\n\nAdding these together: 91,200 plus 7,980 is 99,180. Then, adding 228 gives me 99,408.\n\nWait, that's the same result as before. Maybe I was correct the first time. Let me verify using another method.\n\nI can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n```\n    114\n  x 872\n  -------\n    228   (114 x 2)\n   7980   (114 x 70, shifted one position to the left)\n  91200   (114 x 800, shifted two positions to the left)\n  -------\n  99408\n```\n\nYes, adding these up gives 99,408. So, the correct product of 114 and 872 is 99,408.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 114 and 872?\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet's calculate the product of 114 and 872 step by step.\n\n1. **Multiply 114 by 2 (the ones place of 872):**\n   \\[\n   114 \\times 2 = 228\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 114 by 70 (the tens place of 872):**\n   \\[\n   114 \\times 70 = 7,980\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 114 by 800 (the hundreds place of 872):**\n   \\[\n   114 \\times 800 = 91,200\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the results together:**\n   \\[\n   228 + 7,980 + 91,200 = 99,408\n   \\]\n\n# Answer:\nThe product of 114 and 872 is \\(\\boxed{99,\\!408}\\).",
