      "index": 0,
      "text": "154,368.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Let me double-check my calculations.\n\nStarting over, multiplying 402 by 4 gives 1,608.\n\nMultiplying 402 by 80 gives 32,160.\n\nMultiplying 402 by 300 gives 120,600.\n\nNow, adding these together: 1,608 plus 32,160 is 33,768. Adding 120,600 to that gives a total of 154,368.\n\nHmm, that still doesn't feel correct. Let me try another approach.\n\nAlternatively, I can break down 384 into 300, 80, and 4.\n\nMultiplying 402 by 300 gives 120,600.\n\nMultiplying 402 by 80 gives 32,160.\n\nMultiplying 402 by 4 gives 1,608.\n\nAdding these together: 120,600 plus 32,160 is 152,760. Adding 1,608 gives a total of 154,368.\n\nWait, I'm getting the same result again. Maybe I was correct the first time. Let me verify using another method.\n\nI can also use the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 402 by 384:\n\nFirst, multiply 402 by 4, which is 1,608.\n\nThen, multiply 402 by 80, which is 32,160.\n\nNext, multiply 402 by 300, which is 120,600.\n\nAdding these together: 1,608 plus 32,160 is 33,768. Adding 120,600 gives 154,368.\n\nYes, that seems consistent. So, the correct product of 402 and 384 is 154,368.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 402 and 384?\n\n# Reasoning:\n\nLet's calculate the product of 402 and 384 step by step.\n\n1. **Multiply 402 by 4 (the ones place of 384):**\n   \\[\n   402 \\times 4 = 1,\\!608\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 402 by 80 (the tens place of 384):**\n   \\[\n   402 \\times 80 = 32,\\!160\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 402 by 300 (the hundreds place of 384):**\n   \\[\n   402 \\times 300 = 120,\\!600\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the results together:**\n   \\[\n   1,\\!608 + 32,\\!160 + 120,\\!600 = 154,\\!368\n   \\]\n\n# Answer:\nThe product of 402 and 384 is \\(\\boxed{154,\\!368}\\).",
