batch_outputs:  [["\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 312 and 414?\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet's calculate the product of 312 and 414 step by step.\n\n1. **Multiply 312 by 4 (ones place of 414):**\n   \\[\n   312 \\times 4 = 1,248\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 312 by 10 (tens place of 414):**\n   \\[\n   312 \\times 10 = 3,120\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 312 by 400 (hundreds place of 414):**\n   \\[\n   312 \\times 400 = 124,800\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the results together:**\n   \\[\n   1,248 + 3,120 + 124,800 = 129,168\n   \\]\n\n# Answer:\nThe product of 312 and 414 is \\(\\boxed{129168}\\).", "129,168.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Let me double-check my calculations.\n\nStarting over, multiplying 312 by 4 gives 1,248.\n\nMultiplying 312 by 10 gives 3,120.\n\nMultiplying 312 by 400 gives 124,800.\n\nNow, adding these together: 1,248 plus 3,120 is 4,368. Adding 4,368 to 124,800 results in 129,168.\n\nHmm, that still doesn't match my initial thought. Maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication steps.\n\nLet me try another approach. I'll break down 414 into 400 + 10 + 4.\n\nMultiplying 312 by 400: 312 × 400 = 124,800.\n\nMultiplying 312 by 10: 312 × 10 = 3,120.\n\nMultiplying 312 by 4: 312 × 4 = 1,248.\n\nAdding these together: 124,800 + 3,120 = 127,920. Then, 127,920 + 1,248 = 129,168.\n\nWait, I'm getting the same result again. Perhaps my initial doubt was unfounded. The correct product of 312 and 414 is indeed 129,168.\n"]]
